# Print Area Environmental Monitor

PHP + MySQL + vanilla JS. No CDNs, no external services, no internet
dependency of any kind — everything runs on your XAMPP stack.

## What this actually does

- `ingest.php` — POST endpoint. Takes one temperature/humidity reading,
  computes dew point, logs it.
- `functions.php` — dew point (Magnus formula) and the alarm logic.
- `api_data.php` — GET endpoint the dashboard polls for current values,
  history, thresholds, and alarms.
- `index.html` — dashboard. Plain HTML/CSS/JS, canvas-drawn charts
  (no chart library), polls `api_data.php` every 5 seconds.
- `simulate.php` — generates **synthetic** demo readings so you can see
  the system work before you have a real sensor or DMC-201 feed. Every
  row it writes is tagged `source='simulated'` in the database and
  shown as "SIMULATED DATA" on the dashboard — it can never be confused
  with a real reading.

## Alarm logic — how it actually behaves (tested)

An alarm is **not** raised the instant a reading crosses a limit. It
walks back through recent readings and only opens once the condition
has held continuously for `dwell_seconds` (default 300s = 5 min). This
matters because a single noisy reading crossing 27.01°C is not a real
excursion, and treating it as one would flood you with alarms.

I ran three scenarios against a live instance before shipping this:

1. **Sustained excursion** — temperature held above the limit for
   10 minutes → one alarm row opened, `start_ts` correctly backdated to
   when the excursion actually began (not when the dwell period ended),
   `peak_value` tracked the worst reading.
2. **Recovery** — feeding in-range readings afterward → the alarm row
   closed with the correct `end_ts`, status changed to `cleared`.
3. **Brief blip** — two out-of-range readings 30s apart, well under the
   5-minute dwell → **no alarm row created at all.** This is deliberate.

One alarm = one continuous episode, not one row per bad reading.

## Setup

```bash
mysql -u root -p < schema.sql
```

This creates the `envmonitor` database and a `DEFAULT` threshold row
(23–27°C, 40–70% RH — **replace with the real paste-datasheet limits**,
this is a placeholder). Then create a DB user and either set these as
environment variables or edit `config.php` directly:

```
ENVMON_DB_HOST=localhost
ENVMON_DB_NAME=envmonitor
ENVMON_DB_USER=envmonitor
ENVMON_DB_PASS=<your password>
```

Drop the whole folder into your XAMPP `htdocs`, e.g.
`C:\xampp\htdocs\envmonitor\`, and open
`http://localhost/envmonitor/index.html`.

## Try it with synthetic data first

```bash
php simulate.php              # gentle random walk around setpoint
php simulate.php --excursion  # deliberately drifts high after ~20s,
                               # so you can watch an alarm actually fire
```

Watch `index.html` while this runs. **Delete these rows before treating
anything as real data** — `DELETE FROM readings WHERE source='simulated';`

## Feeding real data in

Once you have either a sensor or read access to whatever the DMC-201
logs to, POST to `ingest.php`:

```bash
curl -X POST http://localhost/envmonitor/ingest.php \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"printer_id":"SMD-01","temperature":25.1,"humidity":45.2}'
```

If you get a bulk CSV export instead of a live feed, write a one-off
PHP or Python script that reads the CSV and POSTs each row with its
original timestamp via the `ts` field — that backfills history and lets
the same alarm logic run against it retroactively.

## What I have NOT done, and why

- **No real limits.** 23–27°C / 40–70% RH in `schema.sql` is a
  placeholder carried over from the original brief. Update the
  `thresholds` table once you have the actual paste datasheet numbers —
  this is a one-row UPDATE, not a code change.
- **No source-vs-target comparison.** This logs one reading per
  printer. If you get access to both a supply-side and enclosure-side
  reading, the schema's `printer_id` field can be reused as a second
  "probe" identity, but the gating logic discussed earlier (only alarm
  on rising humidity if source dew point is actually lower) isn't
  implemented here — it needs two live channels to mean anything, and
  you don't have that yet.
- **No authentication.** Assumed internal network only. Add HTTP basic
  auth or a login if this needs to be reachable outside the plant LAN.
- **No email/SMS alerting**, only in-dashboard display. Wiring alarms
  to an email would be a small addition to `evaluate_alarms()` once
  you have an SMTP relay to use — genuinely easy to add, didn't want to
  guess your mail setup.
